Interpreter: guard the program counter in execOp

DSP::execOp indexes m_opcodeCache with the program counter and calls the
function pointer it finds there. That vector is sized to mem.sizeP(), so a PC
outside valid P memory is an out-of-bounds read of a function pointer, followed
by a call through it: a host-process SIGSEGV with no diagnosis.

The JIT path has guarded exactly this for a long time (g_jitPcGuard ->
onInvalidPC, dsp.h). The interpreter had no equivalent, which is why the same
firmware kills the process on one engine and survives on the other.

This adds the matching check. One compare on a hot path, branch never taken in
normal operation, reusing the JIT's existing onInvalidPC() which grows the
dispatch table when the PC is merely un-grown and otherwise reports full
register state and halts that DSP.

It matters most on iOS, where the interpreter is the only engine available (the
kernel will not map an executable page for a non-entitled process, so a JIT
build fails at runtime) and a call through a garbage pointer is an immediate
app kill with nothing to diagnose. Every wild-PC bug in any firmware now names
its PC and halts one DSP instead of taking the process down.

Found via mmBootFirmwareTest under DSP56K_FORCE_INTERPRETER=1, which crashed
inside execOp; with the guard it halts cleanly reporting
"INVALID PC ff0000 (valid P memory is 0 - 7fffff)".

Verified: dsp56kTestRunner exit 0; MD JIT readiness unchanged (26894/22090);
MD interpreter baseline unchanged (3380).

Apply in the dsp56300 submodule.

--- a/source/dsp56kEmu/dsp.cpp
+++ b/source/dsp56kEmu/dsp.cpp
@@ -536,6 +536,19 @@ namespace dsp56k
 		const TWord currentOp = pcCurrentInstruction;
 		const auto instructionsBefore = m_instructions;
+
+		// SAFETY NET, interpreter side. The JIT path already traps a PC outside
+		// valid P memory (g_jitPcGuard -> onInvalidPC in dsp.h); the interpreter
+		// had no equivalent and indexed m_opcodeCache out of bounds instead, which
+		// is a wild read and a SIGSEGV with no diagnosis. Observed crashing
+		// mmBootFirmwareTest inside execOp. One compare on a hot path, branch
+		// never taken in normal operation.
+		if(ASMJIT_UNLIKELY(currentOp >= m_opcodeCache.size()))
+		{
+			onInvalidPC(currentOp);
+			return;
+		}
+
 		const auto& opCache = m_opcodeCache[currentOp];
